<% content_for :title, "#{@addon.title} — Tinkerex" %>

<%# A migrated addon carries its markup and CSS classes across the port from its old per-addon
    controller/view into this generic shell, but the wide container was never part of the view
    BODY — the legacy Tools::BoxesController's own view opted in with this exact content_for line,
    sitting outside anything a manifest-driven port copies. Silently dropping it doesn't error: the
    page still renders, just 440px narrower, with a multi-column addon UI (a 3-column grid, a tile
    gallery) squeezed into a single column. The manifest is the one place left for an addon to ask
    for this, since the host — not the addon bundle — owns the page chrome. %>
<% content_for :container_class, "container-wide" if @addon.layout == "wide" %>

<% content_for :crumbs do %>
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Tool path">
    <%= link_to "Tools", tools_path %>
    <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    <span aria-current="page"><%= @addon.title %></span>
  </nav>
<% end %>

<%# The title alone: the manifest's `icon` is an emoji by convention and is not drawn anywhere
    (tools/home/_addon_mark, UX audit G1). %>
<div class="page-head"><h1><%= @addon.title %></h1>
  <p class="page-subtitle muted"><%= @addon.description %></p>
</div>

<%#
  AN ADDON THAT CANNOT RUN HERE SAYS SO INSTEAD OF MOUNTING (2026-11-02, UX audit G3). A greyed
  card is not a link, but a pinned rail link, a bookmark or a typed URL still reaches this page, and
  mounting the bundle there produced whatever its first failing call said — "Service request failed
  (404)" for a missing sidecar. The sentence is the gallery card's, `Addon#unavailable_reason`.

  STILL COSMETIC, NOT A GATE (docs/invariants/addons.md): views ask `available?`, and no action in
  Tools::AddonsController does. The asset, service, states and save doors answer for this addon
  exactly as before, so nothing here may be read as an access control. The unavailable branch is at
  the foot of this file.
%>
<% if @addon.available? %>

<% if @addon.permissions.any? %>
  <ul class="addon-permissions muted">
    <% @addon.permissions.each do |permission| %>
      <li>
        <%= Maker::Addons::Permissions.label_for(permission["name"]) %>
        <% if permission["reasoning"].present? %>— <%= permission["reasoning"] %><% end %>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
<% end %>

<%# The whole addon UI mounts here. Every value the addon needs from the host arrives as a data
    attribute, so the bundle never has to know a Rails route or guess at context.

    `service-url` is a BASE, not a full URL to one endpoint: a bundle that talks to a sidecar (its
    manifest's own `services` entry, e.g. "boxes") builds each call as
    `${ctx.serviceUrl}${service}/${path}` — e.g. `${ctx.serviceUrl}boxes/generate`. It ends in "/"
    for exactly that reason (see `service_base_url` in Tools::AddonsController, which derives it from
    the real route rather than a hand-duplicated literal). `states-url` is a complete URL on its own
    (GET it directly) — it has no per-call path to append, unlike `service-url`.

    `data-addon-ctx-value` carries the SAME information as one parsed JSON blob — plus the host
    level and capability map, which have no attribute of their own — built by `addon_ctx` in
    Tools::AddonsController. It is an ADDITION, not a replacement: the per-attribute values below it
    stay, because a bundle built against either shape may be running against this host. %>
<div data-controller="addon"
     data-addon-ctx-value="<%= @ctx.to_json %>"
     data-addon-entry-value="<%= tools_addon_asset_path(@addon.key, @addon.entry) %>"
     data-addon-save-url-value="<%= tools_addon_path(@addon.key) %>"
     data-addon-service-url-value="<%= @service_url %>"
     data-addon-states-url-value="<%= tools_addon_states_path(@addon.key) %>"
     data-addon-folders-value="<%= @folders.to_json %>"
     data-addon-default-folder-value="<%= @default_parent_node_id %>"
     data-addon-default-folder-label-value="<%= @default_folder_label %>"
     data-addon-children-url-value="<%= library_children_path %>"
     data-addon-create-url-value="<%= library_folders_path %>">
  <%# LOADING, THEN THE TOOL OR A SENTENCE (UX audit G3). The loading line is rendered here so it
      shows before any script runs; every shipped bundle replaces the mount's contents as its
      first act, and addon_controller.js removes the line once mount() resolves in case one
      appends instead. A failure empties the mount and shows the block under it, the raw error kept
      in a collapsed <details> for whoever reports it, never as the page's sentence. %>
  <div data-addon-target="mount">
    <p class="addon-loading muted" data-addon-target="loading" role="status">Loading <%= @addon.title %>…</p>
  </div>
  <div class="empty addon-load-failed" data-addon-target="failure" role="alert" hidden>
    <p>Could not load <%= @addon.title %>.</p>
    <p>Reload the page to try again. If it keeps happening, the person running this server can
      check the add-on — <%= link_to "Help", help_path(anchor: "tools") %> says how.</p>
    <details>
      <summary>Details</summary>
      <pre class="addon-load-detail" data-addon-target="detail"></pre>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>

<% else %>
  <div class="empty addon-unavailable">
    <p><%= @addon.unavailable_reason %>.</p>
    <p>The person running this server can set it up — <%= link_to "Help", help_path(anchor: "tools") %>
      says how.</p>
  </div>
<% end %>
